WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.040
A new era has dawned.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.320
A little miracle of technology - the
atomic power stations are going up.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.320
We're bombarded with radiation
all the time...

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.120
...from everyday objects
like a watch.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.720
Most people think that radioactive
waste is some kind of...

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.640
...but in actual fact, it looks a
little bit like this - black glass.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.160
It will be radioactive
for hundreds of thousands of years,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.840
and this is why we really need to
think about its long-term future.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.600
The danger of radioactive waste
is here today,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.840
and goes on and on tomorrow.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.400
In full operation,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.000
it will supply electricity
for a million people.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.720
Climate change, the gases
blamed for global warming,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.320
are pumped out in ever bigger
quantities.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.720
The window of time to mitigate
the climate crisis

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.320
is shrinking by the day, really.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.600
The problem is how to reduce
greenhouse gases.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.520
Nuclear power
has a long proven record

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.960
as a non-fossil energy source.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.120
Nuclear energy is not a solution
to climate change.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.520
Nuclear energy
has a fraught history.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.320
Greeted with optimism in the 1950s,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.120
its reputation was clouded
by atomic bomb tests

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.840
and disasters like Chernobyl,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.720
but as global leaders
work to tackle climate change,

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.640
nuclear power's allure
has come full circle.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.200
Once up and running,
nuclear power stations

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.440
emit no carbon dioxide
or other greenhouse gases,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.800
and nuclear's constant
energy generation sets it apart

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.440
from renewable sources of energy,
whose output can vary.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.320
For the past 60 years or so,
it has provided

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.560
a very important type
of electricity, which we call...

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.360
That's the type of electricity
that guarantees that 24/7,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.240
no matter when you want to
flip on or off your light switch,

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.520
the lights will be there.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.960
The inherent dangers
of nuclear energy

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.880
make it a controversial
energy source,

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.720
and not everyone agrees
it's worth the risks it brings.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.320
Nearly all of our nuclear waste

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.040
is at coastal sites
in one way or the other.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.480
These are locations that are liable
to coastal processes,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.760
storm surges, sea-level rise.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.440
Those are highly vulnerable

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.120
to the worst impacts of
climate change that we can foresee.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.760
I'm concerned
about the dangers it poses.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.800
In 2011, a devastating earthquake
and tsunami in Japan led to meltdown

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.480
at the Fukushima Daiichi
nuclear reactor.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.120
As a result of
the Fukushima accident,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.120
both Japan and Germany,
for different reasons,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.320
have decided to shut down rapidly

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.920
their national
nuclear energy output.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.200
They ramped up coal and gas output

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.880
for a few years after the accident.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.800
Dr Kharecha's research found
that there were health consequences

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.840
linked to this reduction
in the use of nuclear energy,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.160
as both countries increased
their use of fossil fuels

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.880
in subsequent years.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.560
As a direct
yet unintended consequence

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.480
of their decision to drastically
reduce their nuclear power output,

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.200
after the Fukushima accident,
both Japan and Germany together

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:07.520
lost the opportunity to prevent

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.440
over 28,000 air-pollution-induced
premature deaths

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.360
that were as a result
of fossil fuel power use.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.680
Japan is slowly restoring
its nuclear capacity,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.800
while Germany has increased
its renewable energy output

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.840
over the past decade, although that
was accompanied by a steep rise

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.760
in energy prices for the public.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.000
For countries with nuclear
in their energy mix,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.160
such as the UK, Finland, and China,
constructing efficient new reactors

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.880
is both expensive
and time-consuming.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.240
Hinkley Point C, Britain's first
nuclear reactor since 1995,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.280
will cost...

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.280
...at least twice as much
as the London 2012 Olympic Games,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.240
and this figure continues to rise.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.800
Modern nuclear reactors
are designed meticulously,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.360
and built to withstand earthquakes
and other threats,

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.400
including plane crashes, but even
the best designed nuclear reactors

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.400
create radioactive waste,
and that needs careful disposal.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.360
In the UK, we have about four
and a half million cubic metres

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.800
of radioactive waste.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.520
The largest portion of this,
about 96%, is low-level waste.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.480
High-level waste is
very high radioactivity material,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.360
but we have the smallest volume
of this, just less than 1%.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.240
The radiation from
high-level waste is so intense that,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.080
if you were to touch it,

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:32.200
you'd receive a radiation dose about
a million times more than you would

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.840
receive throughout an entire year
just from natural radiation sources.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.320
It's extremely high
for several thousands,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.760
even hundreds of thousands of years,
so, ideally,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.720
where we want to be is in a place
where we can isolate those wastes

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.520
from future populations
in a safe way -

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.560
for example,
digging a very deep hole underground

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:54.080
and disposing of the waste
there forever.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.000
This solution is known as a...

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.480
...where waste is disposed of

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.880
between 200m and 1,000m

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.640
below the Earth's surface.

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.120
Because most people
don't like the idea

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.720
of living next to radioactive waste,
it's been very difficult

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.680
for governments to find a suitable
site, let alone start building one.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.920
Finland is currently the only
country in the world building

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.080
a permanent underground storage
facility for their nuclear waste.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.000
Every nation with nuclear power
has so far stored its waste

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.800
in temporary facilities,
and those facilities are ageing.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.040
I'm not against deep disposal,
because I think ultimately,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.200
we'll have to deal with
our present waste that way,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.920
but that is so far ahead

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:39.840
that the problem here and now
is safe storage.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.200
And all the time
we're waiting for the holy grail

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.600
of a deep disposal facility. The job
of that facility is to deal with

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.080
the massive amounts of nuclear
waste that we already have.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.880
The idea that we should be
piling more and unknown quantities

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.480
for further ahead
is absolutely ludicrous, in my view.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.840
If we don't get our hands on clean
energy and rapidly scale them up

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:07.440
by as much as possible
within the next few decades,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.640
then we're leaving a massive,
massive burden

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.360
on future generations.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.840
We need to completely decarbonise
the electricity sector,

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:18.320
and ideally also
the overall energy sector,

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.440
if we're to avert
the worst impacts

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.280
of the human-caused climate crisis.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.360
As our understanding
of the climate crisis deepens,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:29.640
and energy demands continue to grow,
the necessity that our energy

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.840
comes from non-fossil fuel sources
makes nuclear power hard to ignore.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.960
It is expensive
and it can be dangerous

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.240
if safety protocols
are not followed.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.320
The highly radioactive waste
it generates is hugely challenging

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.280
to store safely, but it also
provides a reliable power baseline,

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.080
keeping the lights from turning off
on a cloudy, windless winter evening

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.400
and meeting countries' energy needs

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.000
as they transition
away from fossil fuels.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.080
And...

